{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-pagevfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:35:50.984005Z","id":"8502193c2b55c3741c509b9fee0183866531e2122fbdb66971dcadb707e15408","body":{"detail":"Limits::default gives every database 64 MiB of SQLite page cache and a 64 MiB dirty set. On PostgreSQL each instance's database adds crates/clax-fleet/src/pages.rs's 32 MiB read cache, and crates/clax-fleet/src/app_data.rs:87 opens each of up to OPEN_STORES (64) artifact stores per instance with the same defaults. deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml limits a pod to 2Gi of memory, and placement (rendezvous hashing) puts instances on pods with no regard to memory, so about 12 instances with warm working sets, or one instance with a few large app data stores, exceed it and the pod is OOM-killed, moving every instance it held.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":112},"end":{"line":133},"content_hash":"07879cab9684a8083eeb021ff9734f425e9b5c2ce3db59af62d58bc49b89689c"},"suggested_fix":"Give the fleet a per-pod memory budget and derive each database's cache from it (smaller defaults for app data stores, e.g. 2 MiB cache and 8 MiB transactions; the instance database sized from budget / running instances), or share one page cache across connections (SQLITE_CONFIG_PAGECACHE / sqlite3_soft_heap_limit64 via PRAGMA soft_heap_limit) so the process has one bound; document the per-instance footprint in docs/kubernetes.md next to the memory limit.","summary":"Per-database memory defaults are not bounded per pod: one instance can use about 160 MiB, and its app data stores up to 64 x 128 MiB more, under a 2Gi pod limit","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep","perf","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-pagevfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T22:39:16.485699Z","id":"0ab0d43d382bdba87ab0e060b2998523b7aa61968a36a9614035259f55c51a3c","body":{"detail":"crates/clax-pagevfs/src/lib.rs:112-133 sets CACHE_BYTES and MAX_TRANSACTION_BYTES to 64 MiB in Limits::default; lib.rs:751-752 open() uses Limits::default. crates/clax-fleet/src/fleet.rs:499 opens the instance database with clax_pagevfs::open, and crates/clax-fleet/src/app_data.rs:87 opens each app data store the same way, up to OPEN_STORES = 64 (app_data.rs:22). crates/clax-fleet/src/pages.rs:28-29 adds 32 MiB of chunk cache per instance. deploy/k8s/postgres/deployment.yaml:97 limits the pod to 2Gi. The figures are upper bounds (SQLite's cache fills only to the working set), so this is the concern recorded, not a defect.","kind":"comment","references":"8502193c2b55c3741c509b9fee0183866531e2122fbdb66971dcadb707e15408","summary":"confirmed: every database, app data stores included, opens with the 64 MiB cache and 64 MiB transaction defaults, with no per-pod budget","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-pagevfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:00:21.356903Z","id":"bef0e121830102848faac6856020c842bbefc6a247ff6834da5402c47ca5cd81","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:6799792e5","summary":"App data stores open with Limits::SMALL (2 MiB cache, 16 MiB transactions); an instance database's cache defaults to 32 MiB; CLAX_FLEET_SQLITE_HEAP_MIB (1024) sets SQLite's process-wide soft heap limit, one bound for every database of a pod; docs/kubernetes.md, Sizing, gives the per-instance footprint. `bazel test //crates/clax-fleet:clax-fleet_bin_test` PASSED.","supersedes":"8502193c2b55c3741c509b9fee0183866531e2122fbdb66971dcadb707e15408","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-pagevfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:08:47.862803Z","id":"91c33a0225e8e9412a2b559343be5ff9a83ffb90c601425f10de52b3180072b7","body":{"detail":"open_with's doc (lib.rs:811-817) promises that reopening a store's file replaces the earlier open 'so a connection left over from an earlier start cannot write into the later one's'. StoredFile::open (lib.rs:827) reads store.size() before the registry lock is taken, and the earlier open is removed from `files` only afterwards (lib.rs:837-840). Until then the earlier connection keeps reading and committing. A commit that lands in that window leaves the new StoredFile with `size` and `stored_size` from before it. SQLite then reads page 1 (the new header, possibly naming more pages than the file size) against the stale size, so it reports SQLITE_CORRUPT or reads the new pages as zeros past the stale end. The stale stored_size hint also makes PgPages::commit skip the size update and the truncation (crates/clax-fleet/src/pages.rs:182-184). A flush already holding the earlier file's Arc (Store::with_file_mut, lib.rs:599-605) can also complete after the replacement. Separately, if Connection::open or a PRAGMA fails after line 837, the earlier connection, which was working, is already cut off. The test reopening_a_stores_file_replaces_the_earlier_connection (crates/clax-pagevfs/src/tests.rs:240-251) checks only a write after the reopen has returned. clax-fleet does not rely on replacement today (each start makes a new PgPages, a different store key, and the lease epoch fences the old one), so the doc's promise is unguarded for the next caller.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":811},"end":{"line":844},"content_hash":"18a0b2ce4c61e5db35b8778d891e2c6f505631f9dd224ef92c0cd715f4bd2dfc"},"suggested_fix":"Under the registry lock, first remove the earlier open from `files` (and take its file's own lock, so an in-flight commit finishes), then read the store's size, and register the new file only after that: two phases with a 'replacing' marker if the size read must stay outside the registry lock. Or narrow the doc to 'the earlier connection must be idle', and add a test that commits on the earlier connection between the size read and the replacement (a PageStore whose size() blocks on a channel).","summary":"open_with reads the store's size before it replaces the earlier open, so the earlier connection's commits in between leave the new connection with a stale size, and a reopen that then fails has already disabled the earlier connection","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","correctness","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-pagevfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:08:47.862843Z","id":"06d51721c4539008a750b8dadc01cf727cfa2202e4cb3651be5083d92014986e","body":{"detail":"PageStore::commit's contract (lib.rs:86-88) says 'On an error, none of it applies'. PgPages::commit (crates/clax-fleet/src/pages.rs:177-230) can get an error after PostgreSQL committed, for example when the connection drops before the COMMIT reply. flush then keeps `stored_size` at the old size (lib.rs:373-376), and SQLite's rollback truncates back to it. The rollback's commit then carries size == stored_size and a keep_below at or above the old chunk count, so PgPages computes resize = false and truncates() = false (pages.rs:182-184). clax_fleet_files keeps the failed transaction's larger size and its extra chunks until some later commit changes the size. A later open (after a move) sees a file larger than SQLite's page count. SQLite tolerates that and truncates at its next commit, so the cost today is wrong metadata and orphaned rows rather than lost data, but the hint the VFS sends is wrong after any ambiguous failure.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":350},"end":{"line":385},"content_hash":"833830b9d4b16e7490c3d6e7bf9578147553a645207195f2549990254c7ed095"},"suggested_fix":"After a commit error, mark the stored size unknown (e.g. Option<u64>, or re-read store.size() before the next commit) so the next Commit forces the size write and the deletion past keep_below; document in PageStore::commit that an error may mean the commit did apply, and that the VFS then sends a commit that restores the state.","summary":"After a failed commit the VFS assumes the store applied none of it, but a networked store cannot guarantee that, and the stale stored_size hint then keeps PgPages from fixing the size and dropping chunks","tags":["review","review:k8s-deep-2","correctness","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-pagevfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:53.482089Z","id":"81113932c2b12caa8b5ae58338e09583fb3b6c0d6bad96c8f31da4b08096e27a","body":{"detail":"StoredFile::open reads store.size() into size and stored_size (lib.rs:207-216), called at lib.rs:827 before registry() is locked; the earlier open leaves r.files only at lib.rs:837-840, so a commit by the earlier connection in between leaves the new StoredFile's size stale. Connection::open_with_flags_and_vfs and the PRAGMAs (lib.rs:845-866) run after the earlier open was removed, so their failure leaves neither connection usable. The doc at lib.rs:813-817 promises replacement without an idle precondition.","kind":"comment","references":"91c33a0225e8e9412a2b559343be5ff9a83ffb90c601425f10de52b3180072b7","summary":"confirmed: open_with reads the size before replacing the earlier open, and cuts the earlier connection off before the new one is known to work","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-pagevfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:53.482333Z","id":"7a652b895987775bdf1c7126d8a5201ced1b09b6221d21f7c03bde9f2da43c3b","body":{"detail":"PageStore::commit's doc says 'On an error, none of it applies' (lib.rs:86-88). StoredFile::flush on error restores dirty and returns without touching stored_size (lib.rs:371-376). PgPages::commit decides resize = size != stored_size || stored_size == 0 and truncate = commit.truncates() (crates/clax-fleet/src/pages.rs:183-184; Commit::truncates at lib.rs:115-117 compares stored_size's chunk count with keep_below), so a restoring commit at the old size, after a PostgreSQL COMMIT that applied but whose reply was lost (pages.rs:229), writes neither the size nor the DELETE of chunks past keep_below. As a suggestion the severity fits: no data is lost.","kind":"comment","references":"06d51721c4539008a750b8dadc01cf727cfa2202e4cb3651be5083d92014986e","summary":"confirmed: after a commit error the VFS keeps the old stored_size, and PgPages then skips the size write and the truncation","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-pagevfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:41.482264Z","id":"3f7f9c2474f8f8f7a8e6e5af06eb8abac182833f2a445fcfb3a7bd77f8e3f79a","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"Commit::stored_size is Option<u64>: after a failed commit the VFS sends None, so the store writes the size and deletes past keep_below; PageStore::commit's doc says an error may mean it applied. Test: clax-pagevfs tests::a_commit_that_failed_leaves_the_stored_size_unknown. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"06d51721c4539008a750b8dadc01cf727cfa2202e4cb3651be5083d92014986e","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/clax-pagevfs/src/lib.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T04:09:41.564856Z","id":"697698459d87087af589ba59d8b4b49c01325c4a35471198114166b77d412b53","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:e6b59f7af","summary":"open_with takes the earlier open out of the registry and holds its file's lock before reading the size; the earlier file becomes PageFile::Replaced on success and is put back on failure. Tests: a_reopen_stops_the_earlier_connection_before_it_reads_the_size, a_failed_reopen_leaves_the_earlier_connection_working. Evidence: `just ci` at e6b59f7af passes the clax-fleet, clax-pagevfs, scripts and manifest tests (357 of 361; the 4 failures are clax-server api_seats::load_150_people_on_100_seats and //apps/clax_app:apk_test, which fail on main too, and two web e2e lanes that passed when rerun alone). PostgreSQL tests are ignored without CLAX_TEST_PG_URL.","supersedes":"91c33a0225e8e9412a2b559343be5ff9a83ffb90c601425f10de52b3180072b7","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:52195316-c20b-49ba-9ad3-4026c7d5a1e0"]}}
